## Stale after the sweep

`vta-webvh/src/webvh_auth.rs` told readers two things that stopped being true this week:

```rust
/// did-hosting accepts this natively (and recognises the historical
/// `affinidi.com/webvh/1.0/authenticate` form during the alias-table
/// migration window).
```

```rust
// did-hosting accepts both this and the historical
// `affinidi.com/webvh/1.0/authenticate` form via its alias
// table during the migration window; once that alias is
// dropped (Phase 3 cleanup on did-hosting) this is the only
// form on the wire.
```

The alias is dropped. `affinidi-webvh-service` #172 removed it from the three REST acceptors and moved their clients; #174 moved the witness's DIDComm router. The canonical URI is now the only form that routes anywhere.

## Why this is worth a PR rather than leaving

**No behaviour change** — this crate has always sent the canonical form, and `authenticate_message_type_is_canonical_spec_uri` already asserted it.

What changes is what a reader believes. The old comment describes a fallback that no longer exists, and the test reads as forward-looking ("once that alias is dropped") when it is now the thing holding the contract in place. A comment that promises a safety net which has been removed is worse than no comment: it is the reason someone doesn't check.

That matters here specifically, because this whole sequence started with a client sending a URI its peer had stopped routing, and the thing that hid it was another surface still quietly accepting the old spelling.

`cargo clippy -p vta-webvh --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean, `fmt --check` clean, 53 tests pass.

Adds a companion to `DRAFTING-RULES.md` describing how task-force members and their AI assistants can back a position with runnable evidence, and links the public evidence repository (github.com/mitchuski/dtgwg-zkp-mage — discussion #14) from the README.

What the document does:
- names the evidence repository's lanes in one line each (per-predicate runtimes, real circuits with published numbers, a conformance-fixture format, a verification registry, and the board cards for #18) and why each matters to the task force;
- states the working practice's three rules — the assistant operates, the member decides; disclose the assistant; say only what the tests hold — so they can be held to;
- draws the path from a discussion to a runnable claim, marking where a person must stand (posting, seat admission, publication);
- says what it is not: not a construction selection, not an audit, not a requirement on anyone.

Scope: two files, docs only (`AGENT-RUNTIMES.md` new; `README.md` +1 line under *Where to start*, +1 section *Evidence and reference runtimes*). No change to the requirements document or the drafting rules. Same IPR posture as the repository (CC BY 4.0).

Offered open-source style: the evidence repository is not a dependency of this one and never will be; it is one member's method, shared into the fold so others can run it, refute it, or replace it through the same gate.

Disclosure: drafted with an AI assistant from the co-chair's working record; reviewed and submitted by the co-chair.

